// probe: n_handle2
#pragma plugin nvmidi

// The array type and the port type, asked for separately, in a script that
// makes no call at all.
//
// n_array.nvgt declares the ports and stops there. This file goes one step
// further and asks the engine for the array type itself, spelled the way the
// plugin spells it, with no port anywhere in the file. If `array<midi_note@>@`
// is refused here, then the array type is the thing that does not survive the
// registration and the port methods were never the fault. If it is accepted,
// the array is fine and the fault is in the port registration itself.
//
// The second question is the array spelling, not the port spelling, and it is
// asked of midi_note - the one type measured to declare cleanly. A port name
// must not appear anywhere in this file: run 36638827355 showed that declaring
// a port in the same translation unit costs midi_note its data type (see
// n_noteonly and n_repeat), and a file that declares both would answer its own
// question with its own contamination.
//
// `array<midi_note@>@ notes` is the by-handle spelling. It is the one that
// stood in the plugin's registration lines until this commit. If it is refused
// here while `midi_note@ n` in the line above is accepted, the array spelling
// is the fault and the port methods were collateral. If it is accepted, the
// array is exonerated and what remains is the port registration itself.
void main() {
	print("HANDLE2_BEGIN");

	print("BLOCK_NOTE");
	midi_note@ n2 = null;
	print("BLOCK_NOTE_OK");

	print("BLOCK_ARRAY");
	array<midi_note@>@ notes = null;
	print("BLOCK_ARRAY_OK");

	print("HANDLE2_END");
}
